        ":question/problem": "Use the substitution $u=x^{3} + 2$. Which integral is obtained?\n\n$$\n\\int x^{2} e^{x^{3} + 2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=x^{3} + 2$. Then $\\mathrm{d}u=(3 x^{2})\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int x^{2} e^{x^{3} + 2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\int e^{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformed integral is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\int e^{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\n$$",
