        "title": "Integrating Products Containing the Exponential Function"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "c22119ed-4d62-59b3-b608-2baf3eae6ef0",
        ":question/problem": "Use the substitution $u=x^{2}$. Which integral is obtained?\n\n$$\n\\int x e^{x^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=x^{2}$. Then $\\mathrm{d}u=(2 x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int x e^{x^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\int e^{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformed integral is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\int e^{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
            ":answer-field/id": "e3937ad0-18a8-5533-ae3d-07a1f29bcd84",
